  "reasoning": "The patient's symptoms include severe stomach pain, constipation, headache, and nightly chills. This combination closely matches the clinical presentation of typhoid fever. Training reference [1] provides a direct precedent, mapping stomach pain, constipation, high fever, and chills to a typhoid diagnosis. Other potential diagnoses do not fit the full symptom profile: malaria involves chills and headaches but lacks the prominent constipation and stomach pain, instead featuring sweating and muscle soreness; peptic ulcer disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease present with burning or acid-related pain triggered by meals or lying down, not constipation and systemic chills; urinary tract infection focuses on urinary urgency and frequency without these specific gastrointestinal and systemic signs. The facts consistently point to a single condition, establishing typhoid as the correct diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]typhoid[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
